{
 "result": "Built csvsum.py: a thin launcher that compiles the embedded C helper (csvsum_c.c) on first use to ./csvsum_c (cached) and os.execv's it, with a pure-Python fallback if no compiler exists. The C program slurps all of stdin in one growing buffer, skips the header line, then single-pass parses each row by locating the commas with memchr, parsing the third field into a double with a hand-rolled parser, and accumulating into a power-of-two open-addressing hash table (FNV-1a hash, linear probing, doubling rehash) keyed on the cat bytes; finally it qsorts the entries so output is sorted by cat and prints \"cat,%.2f\" through a 1 MB buffered writer. Correctness: output is byte-identical to the independent reference (ref.txt generated by the naive defaultdict(float) parser) \u2014 exact on both bench.csv and the alternate test.csv. Speed: the official assay (python3 bench.py) reports 26,038,558 rows/s vs the registered baseline of 3,276,921.6 rows/s, roughly an 8x improvement, with ~0.077 s wall time for 2,000,000 rows (timing spread ~0.05-0.08 s, well within noise).",
 "calls": 16,
 "secs": 711.7
